nodecode-inbox is a rewrite of NousResearch's NousResearch/hermes-labs-plugins:

  https://github.com/NousResearch/hermes-labs-plugins
  commit 4987918a068fcf4b3efbb584db5dd05e5f57c669
  MIT License, Copyright (c) 2026 Nous Research (LICENSE)

Rewritten: the plugin's `hermes inbox` command -- `report.py`'s snapshot and
its text view, and the read-only collectors in `collectors.py` -- as one
Nodecode cell. The five sections are the same five and print in the same
order with the same glyphs: Needs attention (what a cell last said under a
standing-notice key, a session whose answer waits on the person, a cron job
whose last run failed or that was paused), In progress (the evaluations
still running, each with the id the model's own (eval-status N) takes),
Finished, result not yet delivered (a cron answer the session that scheduled
it has not been sent yet), Scheduled (the active jobs and how far away each
next fire is) and Open surfaces (the sessions seen most recently, with how
long ago). The row limit of the section is the one thing the command did not
have. Nothing is written, no source is required, and a source that could
not be read is named in the `Not read` footer and in the snapshot's
:sources instead of failing the read -- the plugin's own posture, on this
harness's stores.

Left out, and why: the stores, because they are Hermes's. `processes.json`
and its psutil liveness re-check, the `async_delegations` table, the
`runtime/active_sessions.json` leases and the shared kanban board have no
counterpart here, so the cell reads this home's own four sources instead --
the standing-notice board, the session directory, the cron cell's registry
row and the image's evaluation registry -- through their public readers.
Also left out: `--json`, since the snapshot is already a plist
(`(NODECODE-INBOX:SNAPSHOT)`) and the text is what a person and a model
read; the `hermes -p PROFILE` flag, which here is another home; and the
`sandboxed` process state, which went with the process registry. Pending
approvals are still not shown, as they were not there -- they live in the
memory of the process that asked; what this cell does report is the other
half a person cares about, a session whose newest answer now waits on them.
